WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.070
The library list has member numbers.

2
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.760
The school list has names.

3
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.610
The librarian wants to know who

4
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.270
has an overdue book, and neither

5
00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.670
list can answer alone.

6
00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.130
Today: joining tables with JOIN and

7
00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.370
LEFT JOIN, and judging the result.

8
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.540
A join puts matching rows side by side.

9
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.970
ON says how they match: the loan's

10
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.630
member id equals the member's id.

11
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.160
A plain JOIN keeps only matches: a

12
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.920
member with no loans disappears.

13
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.620
LEFT JOIN keeps every row of the first

14
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.860
table, and puts NULL where nothing

15
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:42.320
matched.

16
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.700
That makes gaps easy to find.

17
00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.210
Left join members to loans, and keep

18
00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.810
the rows where the loan is NULL: those

19
00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.330
members never borrowed a book.

20
00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.120
Quick check!

21
00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.690
An inner join of members and loans.

22
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.900
What happens to a member who

23
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.460
never borrowed a book?

24
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.690
They're left out, without a word.

25
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.390
If you need everyone, use a left join.

26
00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.490
Joins can multiply rows too.

27
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.260
A member with three loans appears

28
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.460
three times, once per loan.

29
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.720
That's right for a list of loans, but

30
00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.580
wrong if you're counting members.

31
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.990
So judge every result.

32
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.750
Count the rows before and after the join.

33
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:34.510
Did some vanish?

34
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.060
Did some double?

35
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.580
And does the answer match a few

36
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:39.920
rows you checked by hand?

37
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.900
In the app, pupils on one

38
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.440
side, marks on the other.

39
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.520
Try inner and left joins, and watch

40
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.880
who disappears, who appears twice,

41
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.020
and how the count of rows and the

42
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.460
count of marks drift apart.

43
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:55.620
Your turn.

44
00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.080
Kofi sat the test and a resit, and he

45
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.660
appears twice after the join.

46
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.100
Is that a mistake?

47
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.680
No: one row per match is how joins

48
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.440
work, so count with care.

49
00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.770
Remember: a join with ON

50
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.770
matches rows on a key.

51
00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.100
Inner drops what doesn't match;

52
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.120
left keeps it as NULL.

53
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.200
And judge every result: missing

54
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.020
rows, or doubled ones?

55
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.780
Try it tomorrow: explain a join to someone

56
00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.100
at home with two sets of cards, names with

57
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.060
numbers and books with numbers.

58
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.530
Match them up.

59
00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:38.670
Who has no book, and who has two?
